his military career and ultimate fate."}, {"sourceSlug": "eastern-han-dynasty", "sourceName": "Eastern Han Dynasty", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "guan-ping", "targetName": "Guan Ping", "context": "Guan Ping's early military service and rise occurred during the tumultuous final decades of the Eastern Han dynasty."}], "places": [{"name": "Jing Province, China", "role": "Area of Operations"}, {"name": "Fancheng, China", "role": "Major Battle Site"}, {"name": "Linju, China", "role": "Place of Execution"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Guan Ping", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "guan-ping"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q735481", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guan_Ping"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Guan Ping's death in 219 CE, alongside Guan Yu, directly precipitated the loss of Jing Province for Shu Han, a strategic setback that profoundly influenced the subsequent military and political landscape of the Three Kingdoms period. His actions and fate, though secondary, were integral to major regional power shifts.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-14T23:05:23Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29372845654", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Guan Ping was a military general serving under the warlord Liu Bei during the late Eastern Han dynasty of China.", "newValue": "Guan Ping, a pivotal military general, served the warlord Liu Bei during the tumultuous late Eastern Han dynasty and the subsequent Three Kingdoms period (c. 184–280 CE) in ancient China. His significance is primarily tied to his role as the adopted son and trusted subordinate of the revered general Guan Yu, making him an integral, albeit secondary, figure in the foundation of the state of Shu Han. Operating from the strategic Jing Province, Guan Ping's career was defined by military campaigns against rival warlords Cao Cao and Sun Quan, contributing to the initial territorial gains and defensive struggles of Liu Bei's nascent domain.

Guan Ping's military career saw him participate in key engagements that shaped the early Three Kingdoms landscape. He notably accompanied Guan Yu during the arduous defense of Jing Province, a critical strategic nexus contested by all three major powers. In 219 CE, he played a direct role in the Fancheng Campaign, where Guan Yu besieged Cao Ren's forces at Fancheng and Yu Jin's relief army was famously annihilated by a flood. However, this success was short-lived. As Sun Quan launched a surprise attack on Jing Province from the rear, Guan Ping, alongside his adoptive father, was forced to retreat, eventually being captured and executed at Linju by Sun Quan's forces.

Guan Ping's death in 219 CE, alongside Guan Yu, marked a catastrophic turning point for Liu Bei's ambitions, directly leading to the loss of Jing Province, a vital strategic and logistical base for Shu Han. This loss crippled Shu's ability to launch northern expeditions against Cao Wei and severely strained the alliance with Sun Quan's Eastern Wu, culminating in the devastating Battle of Xiaoting (Yiling) in 221-222 CE. His demise shifted the balance of power, contributing to Shu Han's eventual territorial limitations and influencing the strategic calculus of the remaining two kingdoms."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-14T23:05:23Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29372845654", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "178 CE"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "178 CE"}], "newValue": ["The collapse of central authority during the late Eastern Han dynasty created a power vacuum, enabling regional warlords like Liu Bei to rise.", "The strategic importance of Jing Province as a contested territory necessitated a strong military presence and capable commanders under Liu Bei.", "Liu Bei's formation of the Shu Han state required a loyal and effective military hierarchy, drawing upon figures like Guan Ping for command."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-14T23:05:23Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29372845654", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "219 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "219 CE"}], "newValue": ["Guan Ping's death contributed to the critical loss of Jing Province for Shu Han, fundamentally altering the strategic balance of the Three Kingdoms.", "His demise